concept self_parent {
  name     = "self & parent"
  summary  = "Keywords that navigate the enclosing scope — self is the current block, parent the one around it."
  related  = [references, fields_blocks, expressions]
  audience = :both
  body {
    p "Inside a block, the `self` and `parent` keywords are navigators into the document — the same kind of lazy reference a `&T` field holds. `self` resolves to the **current** block (the innermost enclosing scope); `parent` resolves to the block **around** it. Both walk the lexical scope chain upward, so a field can read a sibling or an ancestor without naming the whole path."
    h2 "self"
    p "`self` names the current block. Member access off it reads a field declared in the same block — handy when a name would otherwise be shadowed, or to be explicit about where a value comes from."
    code "wcl" { source = <<'SRC'
box "panel" {
  width  = 480.0
  height = 270.0
  // self.width reads this block's own `width` field.
  ratio  = self.width / self.height
}
SRC
    }
    h2 "parent"
    p "`parent` names the enclosing block one level out. Use it to read a value declared on the container from within a nested block. Referencing `parent` at the document root is an error — there is no scope above it."
    code "wcl" { source = <<'SRC'
service "web" {
  region = "us-east-1"
  metadata {
    // parent.region reaches the surrounding service block.
    inherited_region = parent.region
  }
}
SRC
    }
    callout "Navigators, not copies" { class = ["note"]  body = "self and parent reify to references resolved lazily through lexical scope (innermost block out, then the document root) — the same resolution a `&T` field uses. See [References](concept_references)." }
  }
}
